('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'3. Professional Licensing and Engineer Registration','| Jurisdiction | Licensing Pathway | Key Requirement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Dubai Municipality (DM) | Society of Engineers UAE (SOE) membership + DM card exam | G+1 / G+4 / G+12 / Unlimited categories; card required to sign drawings and supervise sites [[1]](#ref1) |
| Abu Dhabi DMT | Engineers Licensing System (EPLS) on TAMM portal | Minimum 3 years experience; practicing license tied to firm registration [[14]](#ref14) |
| Trakhees / DDA | Trakhees accredited consultant list | Firm accreditation required; individual engineer experience verified |
| SOE UAE Membership | Attested engineering degree + experience record | National professional body membership; required for municipality card in most emirates [[1]](#ref1) |','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'4. FIDIC Contract Framework in UAE Practice','| Form | Common UAE application | Practical note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| FIDIC Red Book 1999 | Employer-designed works; most widely used UAE contract form[[2]](#ref2) | Engineer administers; Particular Conditions amend UAE law conflicts |
| FIDIC Yellow Book 1999 | Design-and-build; contractor responsible for design | Used for MEP turnkey, fit-out, and specialist packages |
| FIDIC Silver Book 1999 | EPC/Turnkey; contractor carries risk | Common in oil & gas, infrastructure concessions |
| FIDIC 2017 Editions | Gaining traction but often heavily amended | 2017 DAB replaced by dispute avoidance/adjudication board |','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'C. UAE fire safety critical defect list','Before Civil Defense final inspection, teams should resolve: non-rated compartmentation penetrations (every hole in a fire wall must be fire-stopped to FM 4991 or UL-listed system); sprinkler heads within 450 mm of a luminaire; missing fire-rated ductwork collars; staircase pressurisation system functional test incomplete; fire pump power supply not on dedicated circuit; fire alarm panel not in approved location; exit sign luminance below code minimum; door-closer adjustment needed on fire doors; emergency lighting duration below 3-hour requirement; and missing fire-rated access panels for active system valves.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Project lifecycle authority coordination checklist','1. **Pre-design (NOC round 1)** — DEWA/ADDC utility capacity NOC; RTA traffic NOC; Etisalat/du telecoms NOC; Civil Defense initial comments; municipality area planning clearance.
2. **Concept design** — Municipality schematic approval; Al Sa''fat/Estidama preliminary registration; specialist NOCs (DGCX, aviation, heritage zone if applicable).
3. **Detailed design** — Building permit application: upload drawings, calculations, BIM model (if required), structural and MEP approvals, and Civil Defense fire drawings.
4. **Construction** — Foundation inspection; structural slab inspections; MEP rough-in inspections; DEWA/ADDC substation and network inspections; periodic municipality safety inspections.
5. **Completion** — Civil Defense final test and completion certificate; DEWA/ADDC permanent connection; DM/DMT Completion Certificate; NOC from municipality for occupancy; utility meter activation.
6. **Post-completion** — As-built drawings submission; O&M manuals; BIM model as-built (if BIM-mandated project); 10-year decennial liability period for structural engineer and contractor under Civil Code.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'6. Technical Notes — Medical Gas and Vertical Transportation','| System | Key Requirement | Standard / Authority |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Medical oxygen | >99.5% purity; primary + secondary + emergency/reserve source; tested to HTM 02-01 / NFPA 99 | DHA HFG Part E_7 (Dubai)[[3]](#ref3) |
| Medical air (4 bar) | Oil-free; dew point ≤-46°C; bacteria-filter mandatory | DOH Abu Dhabi / DHA guidelines |
| Surgical air (7 bar) | Separate supply from medical air; pressure-regulated at manifold | HTM 02-01 / NFPA 99 |
| Vacuum (AGSS) | Anaesthetic gas scavenging mandatory in all OR/procedure rooms | HTM 02-01 |
| Fire lift | Mandatory buildings >15 m occupied floor; EN 81-72 or ASME A17.1 | DBC Part D[[4]](#ref4) |
| Lift capacity | One lift minimum per 4 storeys; max walking distance to lobby 45 m | DBC / ADIBC |','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Template A: Design Review Checklist Framework','A senior engineering design review should confirm: design basis and load assumptions are documented; code edition and amendment dates are stated; all UAE authority requirements have been addressed by reference; specialist sub-consultant inputs are incorporated and coordinated; sustainability compliance (Al Sa''fat or Estidama as applicable) is addressed; fire and life safety interfaces are resolved; and the drawing set matches calculations and specifications. Any deviation from code requires a written technical justification.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Template B: Authority Submission Package','Before submitting to any UAE authority, confirm: the submitting consultant firm holds a valid practising card/approval for the project emirate; drawings are in the required format and coordinate system; all NOCs from upstream authorities are attached; calculations are signed by a registered engineer; the BIM model (if mandated under DM Circular 9-1-3/2026 or Abu Dhabi DMT requirements) is attached in the required format; and fees or bonds have been paid. Incomplete packages are returned without review in most Dubai and Abu Dhabi portals.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Template C: Site Supervision and Inspection Record','For each Witness or Hold point on the ITP, the site supervisor should confirm: the work activity matches the approved shop drawing; material has a valid test certificate or Material Approval Request (MAR); pre-activity conditions (compaction, formwork, earthing, cleanliness) are met; work execution matches approved method statement; post-activity tests are conducted and recorded; Non-Conformance Reports are raised immediately for failures; and the record is signed, dated, and uploaded to the document management system.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('legal','UAE_Environmental_Sustainability_and_Safety_Regulations',null,'4.3 Water Consumption','*   **Average Consumption**: 550 L/person/day (Global average: 170 L).
*   **Sectoral Breakdown**:
    *   **Residential**: 50% (highest per capita in the world).
    *   **Agriculture**: 40% (primarily from groundwater).
    *   **Industrial**: 10%.','legal UAE Environmental Sustainability and Safety Regulations','UAE_Environmental_Sustainability_and_Safety_Regulations.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Template D: Commissioning and Handover Record','The commissioning package should include: signed Commissioning Manager appointment; pre-commissioning mechanical completion certificates; equipment startup records with measured versus specified values; functional test sheets for all systems; cause-and-effect matrix testing records for life-safety interfaces; Training records for the building operator; Operating and Maintenance manuals with as-built BIM model; and a Defect Tracking Register. In Dubai, DEWA/DCD and DM will conduct their own independent inspections before issuing the Completion Certificate.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Template E: Professional Risk Register for UAE Engineering Projects','Key project risks to track: BIM submission readiness; NOC expiry dates and renewal sequences; Specialist material lead times (high-performance concrete, specialist glazing, medical gas equipment); Third-party inspection availability; Civil Defense comments on fire systems; Authority variations discovered late in design; Structural peer review findings; Geotechnical report limitations; Weather restrictions on concrete pours; Power supply sequencing for DEWA connection; District Cooling provider connection timing; and Snagging management strategy for Completion Certificate.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'A. Concrete pour control in UAE climate','Fresh concrete must not exceed 32°C at the time of pouring per Dubai Municipality requirements. Practical controls include: scheduling pours at night or early morning during summer; pre-cooling aggregates and water; using liquid nitrogen injection for very large pours; covering trucks and placing time limits from batching plant to pour; using ice as part of mix water; insulating formwork if mass concrete; and monitoring temperature with embedded thermocouples for pours exceeding 500 mm thickness where heat-of-hydration crack risk exists.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'B. UAE wind loading practice for tall buildings','The Dubai Building Code 2021 references ASCE 7-16 for wind design. For buildings exceeding 120 m height or irregular floor plates, a specialist wind tunnel study is mandatory. The study typically includes: mean wind speed profile; turbulence intensity profile; pressure distribution across facade panels and corners; base shear and overturning moment for structural frame; floor acceleration for occupant comfort under dynamic wind; and glass/cladding pressure coefficients. Wind consultants use a Boundary Layer Wind Tunnel (BLWT) and provide client-specific pressure maps that override ASCE 7 simplified charts.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'D. DEWA final inspection readiness checklist','Before requesting DEWA final inspection for electricity connection: all MDB/SMDB/DB boards installed, labeled, and secured; all cable connections terminated and insulation resistance tested; earth electrode installed, measured, and result ≤1 ohm; all circuits identified, protected, and balanced within 10% between phases; emergency power circuits segregated and labeled; RCDs tested; cable trunking/trays fully supported and bonded; substation ventilation confirmed; and as-built single line diagram reflects installed equipment.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'E. Geotechnical risk register for UAE projects','Common geotechnical risks in UAE: (1) Sabkha — expansive salt-rich soils that lose bearing capacity when wetted; mitigate by removal and replacement or ground improvement; (2) Gypsiferous soils — dissolution creates voids under foundations; test sulfate content and use sulfate-resistant cement; (3) Loose sand over the water table — liquefaction risk in seismic events; assess and improve if Vs30 < 180 m/s; (4) High groundwater — dewatering impacts on adjacent structures; pre-construction settlement monitoring required; (5) Coral limestone (caprock) — variable quality; carry out borehole core quality designation (RQD) and end-bearing verification for piles; (6) Contaminated land — former industrial or oil storage sites; Phase 1 and Phase 2 assessment before construction.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'F. UAE project documentation matrix','For all UAE engineering projects, the following documentation set should be maintained: Design Brief + Basis of Design memo; Approved shop drawings register; Material Approval Request (MAR) log; Inspection and Test Plan (ITP) with sign-off sheets; Non-Conformance Report (NCR) register; Request for Information (RFI) log; Authority submission log with NOC expiry tracking; As-built drawing issue record; Commissioning test sheets; O&M manual receipt confirmation; and Training attendance records. Completeness of this set is assessed during the Completion Certificate audit.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'FIDIC Contract Administration in UAE','The UAE Civil Code and Commercial Transactions Law apply to all UAE contracts. Key intersections with FIDIC:
- **FIDIC Sub-Clause 2.5 (Employer''s Claims):** UAE law does not recognise time bars as strictly as FIDIC; UAE courts may allow late claims if the claimant can demonstrate entitlement. Particular Conditions often modify this Sub-Clause.
- **FIDIC Sub-Clause 8.4 (Extension of Time):** The Engineer must determine entitlement within 42 days; UAE practice often sees this provision triggered for adverse weather (sandstorm, rain stoppage), authority delays (NOC), and design changes.
- **Sub-Clause 20 Disputes:** FIDIC 1999 DAB (Dispute Adjudication Board) is often deleted or replaced by a Dispute Resolution Board (DRB) or referral directly to arbitration (DIAC — Dubai International Arbitration Centre, or ADCCAC — Abu Dhabi Centre for Conciliation and Arbitration).
- **Variation orders:** All variations must be instructed in writing; oral instructions must be confirmed within 7 days or they are not valid instructions under standard FIDIC.
- **Retention:** Typically 10% retained up to a limit of 5% of Contract Price; released 50% at Taking Over, 50% at end of Defects Notification Period (typically 1 year).','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'UAE Decennial Liability','Under UAE Civil Code Article 880, designers and contractors are jointly liable for the structural safety of a building for 10 years after completion:
- **Scope:** Structural failures and defects that threaten the building''s stability or safety; applies to structural engineer, architect, and main contractor.
- **Coverage:** Professional Indemnity (PI) insurance and Decennial Liability (DL) insurance are typically required by the employer; some authorities require proof of coverage before Completion Certificate.
- **Exclusions:** Normal wear and tear, owner modifications, and events of force majeure.
- **Practical impact:** Structural engineers should retain all design calculations, approval records, and site inspection reports for the full 10-year period plus legal dispute allowance.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('legal','UAE_Environmental_Sustainability_and_Safety_Regulations',null,'5.2 Municipal Solid Waste (MSW) Composition (% by weight)','*   **Food Waste**: 45% (major focus for reduction).
*   **Paper & Cardboard**: 17%.
*   **Plastics**: 12%.
*   **Metals**: 3%.
*   **Glass**: 2%.
*   **Textiles**: 3%.
*   **Other (Wood, Rubber, etc.)**: 18%.','legal UAE Environmental Sustainability and Safety Regulations','UAE_Environmental_Sustainability_and_Safety_Regulations.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Construction Programme and Delay Analysis','Key elements of a UAE-compliant delay analysis:
- **Baseline programme:** Submitted and approved within the contract timeframe (typically 28 days from commencement); use Primavera P6 or MS Project; must show critical path, key milestones, and resource loading.
- **Programme updates:** Monthly updates required; baseline vs. as-built comparison for delay analysis.
- **Delay analysis methods:** Time Impact Analysis (TIA) or As-Planned vs. As-Built; TIA is preferred by DRBF and FIDIC guidance because it evaluates delays contemporaneously.
- **Concurrent delay:** UAE courts generally require the party claiming extension of time to prove their delay was the dominant cause; pure concurrent delay arguments are less successful than in English law.
- **Prolongation costs:** Documented against actual cost records; site establishment costs, supervision, equipment standing time, and head-office overhead (Hudson formula or Emden formula acceptable).','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Authority Coordination Master Schedule','For a typical Dubai mixed-use tower, the NOC and approval sequence is:
1. Concept design → DEWA capacity study (8–12 weeks); RTA traffic impact study (6–10 weeks); Civil Defense concept review (4–6 weeks).
2. Schematic design → DM schematic approval; DEWA NOC; RTA NOC; telecom NOCs.
3. Detailed design → DM Building Permit (target 2 working days for complete submission); DCD fire drawings approval (2–6 weeks).
4. Foundation → DM foundation inspection; geotechnical report approval.
5. Structure → DM structural inspection at slab levels.
6. MEP → DEWA substation inspection; DCD fire system inspection.
7. Completion → DCD Completion Certificate → DM Completion Certificate → DEWA permanent connection → OC issued.

Each step has typical elapsed times and potential delay risks that should be tracked in the project programme with float assigned.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Extension of Time — UAE Practice Notes','Under FIDIC contracts in the UAE, extensions of time claims must be submitted promptly and supported by contemporaneous records:
- **FIDIC Sub-Clause 20.1 notice:** Notice of intention to claim must be given within 28 days of the event giving rise to the claim; UAE courts have mixed records on enforcing this bar strictly.
- **Programme impact evidence:** Show delay event on approved baseline programme; demonstrate critical path impact using TIA methodology.
- **Authority delays as grounds:** Delays in obtaining NOC, municipality inspection, utility connection, or Civil Defense approval are generally accepted grounds for EOT if the contractor is not responsible.
- **Weather claims:** Exceptional adverse weather (defined in the Particular Conditions, typically with reference to a historical meteorological record) is an excusable delay; UAE sandstorms and heavy rain events qualify if they exceed the defined threshold.
- **Concurrent delay:** If contractor-caused and employer-caused delays run concurrently, the contractor is typically entitled to time only (not prolongation costs) unless the Particular Conditions state otherwise.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Claims Preparation — Document Requirements','A well-prepared UAE construction claim includes:
1. **Narrative:** Factual description of the event, cause, and effect on the works; written in simple clear language.
2. **Programme analysis:** Baseline programme + delay event impact + revised programme showing critical path effect.
3. **Daily records:** Site diary entries, weather records, workforce and plant allocation sheets for the delay period.
4. **Correspondence:** Emails, RFIs, authority letters, variation instructions, and approval notifications that evidence the cause and the contractor''s response.
5. **Quantification:** Build-up of time (days affected) and cost (if applicable) with supporting invoices, timesheets, and overhead allocation.
6. **Legal basis:** Reference to the applicable contract clause(s).','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'UAE Contractor and Consultant Grade Classification','Dubai Municipality classifies contractors by grade (G1–G5, Unlimited) based on financial capacity and project experience. Key points:
- **Unlimited grade:** Required for buildings above G+12; requires minimum AED 50 million annual turnover and substantial project experience.
- **Specialist categories:** Separate licenses for piling, facade, MEP, and fit-out works; a general contractor cannot self-perform specialist works without the relevant sub-license.
- **Consultant grades:** Similar grading for consulting firms; the grade restricts the maximum building height and BUA the consultant can sign off on.
- **Engineer in charge:** An individual licensed engineer (DM card holder) must be named as Engineer in Charge for every permitted project; they carry personal professional liability.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Commissioning and Handover Framework (UAE)','A comprehensive UAE commissioning programme structure:
- **Phase 1 (Pre-commissioning):** Confirm all systems are mechanically complete; leak tests; electrical insulation checks; equipment uncrating and alignment.
- **Phase 2 (Commissioning):** Systems started up individually; set-points adjusted; balancing of air and water distribution; initial functional tests.
- **Phase 3 (Integrated systems testing):** Fire and life safety cause-and-effect tests; BMS sequence of operations verification; emergency power switchover tests; evacuation alarm tests witnessed by Civil Defense.
- **Phase 4 (Performance testing):** Energy benchmarking; IAQ measurements; acoustic measurements; confirm sustainability credits where required.
- **Phase 5 (Handover):** O&M manual delivery; training records; as-built drawings; BIM model as-built; CAFM database population; spare parts handover; defects list agreement.','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
('procurement','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts',null,'Professional Quick Reference: Common UAE Engineering Thresholds','| Parameter | Value | Authority/Source |
|---|---|---|
| BIM mandate threshold (Dubai) | G+12 or ≥15,000 m² BUA | DM Circular 9-1-3/2026 |
| Boreholes for high-rise (Dubai) | Min 5; 1 per 750 m² | Admin Resolution 37/2021 |
| Max shoring lateral displacement (Trakhees) | 40 mm | Trakhees Grey Code |
| Wind tunnel test threshold | ≥120 m height or irregular plan | DBC 2021 |
| Max fresh concrete temperature at pour | 32°C | Dubai Municipality |
| Min concrete cover — foundations | 75 mm | DBC 2021 |
| Max w/c ratio — severe exposure | 0.40 | DBC 2021 / ACI 318 |
| DEWA earth electrode resistance | ≤1 Ohm | DEWA Regulations 2017 |
| Emergency lighting duration | 3 hours (180 min) | UAE Fire Code 2018 |
| Sprinkler smoke detector spacing | Max 9 m | UAE Fire Code |
| Fire lift mandatory above | 15 m occupied floor height | DBC Part D |
| Residential water demand | 250–350 L/capita/day | DEWA Water Planning Guidelines |
| Min self-cleansing sewer velocity | 0.75 m/s | ADSSC Design Guidelines |
| NFPA 285 cladding test | Required all combustible assemblies >2 storeys | UAE Civil Defense |
| Midday break period | 12:30–15:00, 15 Jun–15 Sep | MOHRE |
| Decennial liability period | 10 years post-completion | UAE Civil Code Article 880 |
| FIDIC EOT notice period | 28 days from triggering event | FIDIC Red Book Sub-Clause 20.1 |
| Traffic study threshold (Dubai RTA) | >100 vehicle trips/peak hour | RTA eNOC portal |
| Missing parking space fine (Abu Dhabi) | AED 50,000/space | ITC Abu Dhabi |
| Estidama mandatory minimum rating | 1 Pearl (government: 2 Pearls) | Abu Dhabi DMT |
| Al Sa''fat mandatory minimum rating | Silver Sa''fa | Dubai Municipality |
| ADNOC pressure vessel standard | AGES-SP-06-002 / ASME VIII | ADNOC Group Engineering Standards |
| Hazardous area cert UAE | ECAS-Ex Certificate required | MoIAT ECAS scheme |','procurement 06 Specialist Services Project Management and Contracts','06_Specialist_Services_Project_Management_and_Contracts.md'),
