('legal','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts',null,'5.1 Federal Law No. 11 of 2023 — nine chapters','Lexis'' preview table of contents lists the law as: Chapter 1 Definitions and Objectives (Articles 1–2); Chapter 2 Scope of Application and Exceptions (Articles 3–4); Chapter 3 Governance and Competencies (Articles 5–7); Chapter 4 Procurement System and Purchasing Principles (Articles 8–15); Chapter 5 Purchase Provisions and Methods (Articles 16–22); Chapter 6 Awarding Provisions and Controls (Articles 23–29); Chapter 7 Contracting Provisions and Controls (Articles 30–37); Chapter 8 Considering Complaints (Article 38); Chapter 9 General Provisions (Articles 39–45) [[12]](#ref12).

| Chapter | Articles | Detailed construction relevance |
|---|---:|---|
| 1. Definitions and objectives | 1–2 | Defines UAE, Government, Ministry, Federal Entities, procurement, procurement process, annual plan, emergency procurement, procurement system, public interest, public value, supplier, participating supplier, confidential and sensitive commercial information, tender announcement and public tender. The objectives include digital procurement, equality, fair treatment, non-discrimination, integrity, transparency, competition, public value, SME support, sustainable development and national economy objectives [[11]](#ref11). |
| 2. Scope and exceptions | 3–4 | Applies to procurement/contracting of federal entities, with exceptions requiring article-by-article verification from the official PDF. Defence/security and strategic entities may use separate regimes. |
| 3. Governance and competencies | 5–7 | Establishes governance, delegation matrix and authority controls. For mega-projects, approvals and financial limits are critical. The research brief records ministerial approval for procurements above AED 250M; verify against implementing matrix for live tenders. |
| 4. Procurement system and purchasing principles | 8–15 | E-procurement, purchasing principles, transparency, publication, confidentiality, fair competition and audit trails. |
| 5. Purchase provisions and methods | 16–22 | Open tender, limited tender, direct contracting, emergency procurement, sole source and other methods. |
| 6. Awarding provisions and controls | 23–29 | Evaluation committees, technical/financial scoring, award recommendation, best value and rejection controls. |
| 7. Contracting provisions and controls | 30–37 | Contract execution, guarantees, performance security, variations, extensions, default and close-out. |
| 8. Considering complaints | 38 | Procurement complaint mechanism; the research brief requested a 10-day filing / 15-day review workflow, but live users must verify current implementing regulation and tender conditions. |
| 9. General provisions | 39–45 | General offences, transitional rules, implementation and publication. The user-requested Article 52 penalty reference was not verified in the chapter map; because Lexis preview shows only Articles 1–45, any Article 52 penalty table should be treated as unverified for this law unless a later implementing regulation contains it [[12]](#ref12). |','legal UAE Construction Laws and Contracts','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts.md'),
('legal','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts',null,'5.2 Key requested procurement articles and controls','| Requested item | Deep research treatment |
|---|---|
| Art. 1 definitions | Definitions extracted from MoF PDF include procurement authority concepts through Federal Entity, procurement process, procurement annual plan, emergency procurement, procurement system, public interest, public value, supplier, participating supplier and tender announcement [[11]](#ref11). |
| Art. 4 scope/exclusions | Covered under Chapter 2; verify exact exclusions in official PDF before relying. |
| Art. 6 authority thresholds | Delegation matrix governs financial/non-financial approvals; the brief''s AED 250M ministerial threshold is recorded as a working procurement threshold subject to live matrix confirmation. |
| Art. 8 transparency | Chapter 4 procurement system/purchasing principles supports transparency, digital system and fair competition. |
| Art. 12 open tender | Open tender steps should include procurement plan, tender announcement, tender documents, clarification, bid submission, opening, technical/financial evaluation, award and publication. |
| Art. 17 direct contracting | Typical conditions: sole source, emergency, follow-on compatibility, IP/technical exclusivity and documented justification. |
| Art. 20 technical/financial evaluation | For complex works a 60/40 technical/financial weighting is common; for simple commodities 30/70 may be used. Tender documents control actual weight. |
| Art. 25 framework agreements | Frameworks are suitable for repetitive works/services; the brief records a maximum 4-year framework with mini-competitions for call-offs, subject to implementing regulation. |
| Art. 32 performance guarantee | Performance guarantees commonly 5–10% in UAE construction and government procurement, usually unconditional UAE bank guarantee. |
| Art. 40 disputes | Complaint committee and court appeal route should be checked against Chapter 8/implementing regulations. |
| Art. 52 penalties | Not verified in the located Law 11/2023 table of contents; do not rely without a separate regulation/source. |','legal UAE Construction Laws and Contracts','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts.md'),
('legal','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts',null,'5.3 Dubai procurement and RTA','Dubai government procurement is commonly run through DGEP/e-tender systems and Dubai Law No. 6 of 1997 as amended. The research brief records a local preference of 5% for Dubai-registered firms and a minimum three-member evaluation committee, but those values should be checked against the live tender and procurement manual. RTA supplier policy states that vendors may be subject to prequalification when entering supplies/contracts above AED 1 million [[16]](#ref16). Reuters reported the Dubai Metro Blue Line award at AED 20.5 billion / USD 5.6 billion in December 2024, illustrating the scale of RTA procurement [[17]](#ref17).','legal UAE Construction Laws and Contracts','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts.md'),
('legal','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts',null,'5.4 ADNOC / National ICV','MoIAT''s ICV formula page gives a formula including **((Value of Purchase x ICV of supplier) + Emirati Cost + 60% of Expat Cost) / Total Cost**, plus an asset component based on UAE asset value / total asset value [[15]](#ref15). The research brief expands this for construction bids as:

```text
ICV Score = (Total Emirati Employee Cost × 1.0
           + Total Non-Emirati Employee Cost in UAE × 0.60
           + Total Asset Depreciation in UAE × 1.0
           + Total Purchase of Goods/Services in UAE with ICV certification × ICV Certificate %)
           / Total Revenue × 100
```

Practical ADNOC/ICV controls:

- ICV certificate is based on audited financial data and must be issued by approved certifying bodies; Big Four and major UAE audit firms are commonly used.
- The research brief records a **30% ICV score target** for many ADNOC contracts and ICV improvement plans for contracts over **AED 25M**; verify tender-specific ICV rules.
- Contractors should flow ICV requirements to subcontractors and suppliers, because Tier 1 ICV can be affected by uncertified vendors.
- Baseline ICV submissions should be included with bid; final ICV reporting often occurs at contract completion or within a post-completion period stated in the contract.

---','legal UAE Construction Laws and Contracts','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts.md'),
('legal','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts',null,'6.1 DIAC 2025 fee schedule','DIAC''s 2025 Table states that fees are displayed in USD for illustration and payment directions are issued in AED; the registration fee under Articles 4 and 5 is **USD 3,000** and is non-refundable [[13]](#ref13).

| Amount in dispute | Arbitrator fee per arbitrator | Administrative fee |
|---:|---:|---:|
| to USD 25,000 | USD 2,000–8,000 | USD 3,000 |
| USD 50,001–100,000 | min USD 3,000 + 5% above 50,000; max USD 9,000 + 5% above 50,000 | USD 4,500 + 2.73% above 50,000 |
| USD 500,001–1,000,000 | min USD 15,000 + 1% above 500,000; max USD 36,720 + 3% above 500,000 | USD 15,000 + 0.99% above 500,000 |
| USD 1,000,001–2,000,000 | min USD 20,000 + 0.8% above 1,000,000; max USD 52,920 + 2.3% above 1,000,000 | USD 23,000 + 0.625% above 1,000,000 |
| USD 2,000,001–5,000,000 | min USD 28,000 + 0.4% above 2,000,000; max USD 80,640 + 1.4% above 2,000,000 | USD 30,000 + 0.438% above 2,000,000 |
| USD 5,000,001–10,000,000 | min USD 40,000 + 0.2% above 5,000,000; max USD 127,680 + 0.5% above 5,000,000 | USD 43,000 + 0.175% above 5,000,000 |
| USD 10,000,001–50,000,000 | min USD 50,000 + 0.05% above 10,000,000; max USD 159,850 + 0.2% above 10,000,000 | USD 52,000 + 0.063% above 10,000,000 |

For a USD 2 million dispute, Aceris reports DIAC administrative fees of USD 29,250 and per-arbitrator fees of USD 28,000–75,920 under the 2025 table [[14]](#ref14). For the requested USD 2.7M / AED 10M example, the brief''s calculation of around USD 33,625 admin fee is directionally consistent with the USD 2M+ tier, but exact calculation should use the live calculator/table.','legal UAE Construction Laws and Contracts','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts.md'),
('legal','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts',null,'6.2 AED 50M construction dispute cost example','| Cost head | Typical range / note |
|---|---|
| Registration | USD 3,000 non-refundable [[13]](#ref13) |
| DIAC administrative fee | Approximately USD 52,000 + 0.063% above USD 10M for USD 10M–50M range; for AED 50M (~USD 13.6M), around USD 75k–85k is a conservative planning range after expenses/VAT assumptions |
| Arbitrator fees | For a 3-arbitrator tribunal, often USD 180k–360k depending claim size and court determination |
| Delay/quantum experts | AED 100k–500k each for major disputes; mega-project disputes can exceed this |
| Legal fees | AED 500k–3M+ depending firm, hearing length, document volume and expert intensity |
| Total cost | Often 5–15% of claim value for hard-fought construction arbitration, excluding internal management time |','legal UAE Construction Laws and Contracts','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts.md'),
('legal','UAE_Construction_Laws_and_Contracts',null,'6.3 Expert evidence: delay, quantum and defects','Delay analysis methods used in UAE disputes often follow SCL Protocol categories:

1. As-Planned vs As-Built — simple but vulnerable where baseline unrealistic.
2. Impacted As-Planned — inserts delay events into baseline; useful early but often criticised if not reality-tested.
3. Time Impact Analysis — preferred for contemporaneous prospective analysis when reliable updates exist.
4. Collapsed As-Built — removes employer delays from as-built programme; useful retrospectively but assumption-heavy.
5. Windows Analysis — divides project into windows and studies critical path movement; strong where monthly updates exist.

Quantum experts prepare measured mile productivity analysis, loss and expense claims, prolongation cost, disruption cost, acceleration cost, variation valuation, final account, Scott Schedule defect matrices and repair-cost models. RICS is the dominant professional body for quantity surveyors in the UAE market; the research brief records 4,000+ UAE RICS members and RICS dispute resolution services as relevant market infrastructure.

