('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'10.1 Core Administrative Profile','| Emirate | Capital / Seat | Approximate Area | Main Urban Centres | Economic Profile | Key Professional Notes |
|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|
| Abu Dhabi | Abu Dhabi City | ~67,000 km² | Abu Dhabi, Al Ain, Ruwais, Madinat Zayed, Liwa | Oil and gas, sovereign investment, heavy industry, ports, energy, aerospace, culture, federal government | Largest emirate by area; controls most hydrocarbon reserves; major construction regulatory routes pass through DMT/TAMM, ADDC/AADC, Civil Defence, EAD and ADREC/Tawtheeq. |
| Dubai | Dubai City | ~4,114 km² | Deira, Bur Dubai, Jumeirah, Marina, JLT, Business Bay, Jebel Ali, Hatta | Trade, logistics, aviation, tourism, real estate, finance, technology, retail | Highly portal-driven: DM, DET, DLD/RERA, RTA, DEWA, DCD, Trakhees, DIFC, VARA and numerous free-zone authorities. |
| Sharjah | Sharjah City | ~2,590 km² | Sharjah City, Khor Fakkan, Kalba, Dibba Al Hisn, Al Dhaid | Culture, education, manufacturing, ports, logistics, publishing, affordable housing | Three-coast/emirate geography; important east-coast port at Khor Fakkan; municipality and SEWA approvals central to projects. |
| Ajman | Ajman City | ~259 km² | Ajman City, Masfout, Manama | Manufacturing, real estate, services, port activity, SME licensing | Compact urban jurisdiction; Ajman Free Zone and municipal approvals important for SME and light-industrial operators. |
| Umm Al Quwain | Umm Al Quwain City | ~720 km² | UAQ City, Falaj Al Mualla | Fisheries, light industry, tourism, wetlands, logistics | Lower-density emirate with sensitive coastal lagoons and mangroves; environmental and municipal checks are material for development. |
| Ras Al Khaimah | Ras Al Khaimah City | ~2,486 km² | RAK City, Al Hamra, Julfar, Khatt, Jebel Jais area | Tourism, quarries, cement, ceramics, real estate, ports, RAKEZ | Mountain geology, quarrying and tourism dominate many technical issues; seismic and slope/geotechnical considerations are more important than in Dubai/Abu Dhabi lowlands. |
| Fujairah | Fujairah City | ~1,450 km² | Fujairah City, Dibba, Masafi, Mirbah | Oil bunkering, ports, logistics, quarrying, tourism, east-coast trade | Only emirate primarily on Gulf of Oman; strategic port outside Strait of Hormuz bottleneck; wadi flood risk and mountain geology influence engineering. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'10.2 Emirate Regulatory Orientation for Professionals','| Professional Need | Abu Dhabi | Dubai | Sharjah | Northern Emirates |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Building permit | DMT / municipality through TAMM | Dubai Municipality; Trakhees for PCFC areas | Sharjah Municipality and planning authorities | Local municipality portals and emirate-specific NOCs |
| Utility connection | ADDC / AADC, DoE oversight | DEWA | SEWA | FEWA / Etihad Water & Electricity, local water/electricity authorities |
| Fire safety | Abu Dhabi Civil Defence | Dubai Civil Defence | Sharjah Civil Defence | Emirate Civil Defence branches |
| Property registration | ADREC / DMT | Dubai Land Department / RERA | Sharjah Real Estate Registration Department [verify] | Emirate land departments [verify] |
| Environmental approvals | EAD for Abu Dhabi; MOCCAE for federal/cross-border matters | Dubai Municipality environment sections; MOCCAE for federal matters | EPAA / municipality [verify] | MOCCAE plus local municipality/environment authority |
| Labour / immigration | MOHRE plus ICP / TAMM services | MOHRE plus GDRFA Dubai / Amer | MOHRE plus ICP | MOHRE plus ICP |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'11.1 Landforms and engineering implications','The UAE is often described simply as a desert state, but for planning, engineering, insurance and environmental review it is better understood as a set of sharply different landform systems. The low-lying Arabian Gulf coastal plain includes sabkha flats, reclaimed islands, urban waterfronts, mangrove lagoons and artificially dredged channels. The central and southern interior is dominated by dune fields, gravel plains and the Liwa / Rub'' al Khali desert transition. The eastern margin rises into the Al Hajar mountain system, with wadis, alluvial fans, gabbro, peridotite and other ophiolitic rocks. Each landform creates a different technical risk profile.

| Landform | Where found | Practical implications | Typical professional checks |
|---|---|---|---|
| Coastal sabkha | Abu Dhabi coast, Dubai lowlands, lagoons, industrial reclaimed areas | Shallow saline groundwater, sulfate/chloride attack, settlement risk, aggressive exposure class for concrete and steel | Geotechnical investigation, groundwater chemistry, sulfate-resistant cement, pile design, waterproofing, corrosion protection, dewatering method statement |
| Reclaimed island / dredged fill | Palm Jumeirah, Yas, Saadiyat, Al Maryah, port expansions | Variable compaction, long-term settlement, liquefaction screening, marine environmental approvals | CPT/SPT logs, vibro-compaction records, settlement monitoring, marine EIA, coastal modelling |
| Dune sand | Dubai desert belt, Abu Dhabi western region, Liwa | Wind erosion, drifting sand, low cohesion, access-road burial, heat-island exposure | Sand-control barriers, road alignment studies, compaction and stabilisation, dust management plan |
| Gravel plains / alluvial fans | Al Ain, RAK, Fujairah foothills | Flash-flood channels, boulder wash, variable bearing strata | Wadi hydrology, flood return period, culvert sizing, setback from wadi channels |
| Mountain rock slopes | RAK, Fujairah, Hatta, Khor Fakkan | Rockfall, slope instability, blasting and quarry controls, seismic design checks | Rock mass rating, slope stabilisation, catch fences, retaining systems, drainage channels |
| Offshore islands / platforms | Das, Zirku, Arzanah, Abu Dhabi offshore fields | Marine corrosion, logistics access, environmental sensitivity, emergency response | Offshore safety case, marine transport plan, oil spill plan, corrosion protection and cathodic systems |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'11.2 Climate data useful for design and operations','The UAE''s climate is hot-desert, but operational risk varies by coast, interior and mountain zone. Coastal cities have high humidity during summer; interior deserts have larger day-night temperature swings; mountain and east-coast locations have higher rainfall and flash-flood exposure. For building design, the most operationally important climate factors are peak dry-bulb temperature, coincident wet-bulb temperature, humidity, solar radiation, dust loading, corrosive marine air, and the winter rainfall pulses that generate wadi floods.

| Climate factor | Typical UAE pattern | Professional relevance |
|---|---|---|
| Summer heat | Long hot season, with daytime temperatures commonly above 40°C in peak months | HVAC sizing, labour midday-break compliance, materials storage, concrete curing, asphalt work planning |
| Humidity | High on Arabian Gulf coast in summer, lower in inland desert | Condensation control, mould prevention, latent cooling load, façade dew-point checks |
| Rainfall | Low annual totals but concentrated winter events; mountains receive higher totals | Stormwater design, wadi crossings, basement flood protection, roof drainage, emergency response |
| Dust / sand | Shamal winds and local dust events can sharply reduce visibility and raise particulate matter | Air filtration, façade maintenance, PV panel cleaning, airport operations, worker respiratory protection |
| Marine salinity | Arabian Gulf has high salinity and high evaporation; Gulf of Oman conditions differ | Desalination, cooling water intake, corrosion, marine ecology, concrete exposure classes |
| Solar radiation | High year-round, especially in open desert | PV yield, façade heat gain, shading, roof SRI, outdoor thermal comfort |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'12.4 Post-federation development chronology','| Decade | Key developments |
|---|---|
| 1970s | Federation institutions built; oil revenues funded roads, housing, schools, hospitals and ports; UAE dirham introduced; ADNOC and key state institutions consolidated. |
| 1980s | GCC founded; Jebel Ali Free Zone and Emirates airline created; Dubai''s trade/logistics model accelerated; Abu Dhabi continued hydrocarbon and sovereign investment strategy. |
| 1990s | Gulf War security environment; Dubai tourism and real-estate positioning strengthened; free-zone model expanded; ports and airports became regional hubs. |
| 2000s | Burj Khalifa, Palm Jumeirah, DIFC, ADGM planning, global property boom and 2008–2009 crisis; Abu Dhabi and Dubai bailout/restructuring arrangements became central to financial history. |
| 2010s | Expo 2020 awarded; major legal reforms; nuclear programme construction; renewable-energy mega-projects; UAE increasingly active in diplomacy, aid and regional security. |
| 2020s | COVID-19 response, Mars Hope Probe, Abraham Accords, Expo 2020 Dubai, COP28, FATF grey-list exit, corporate tax implementation, rail network launch, major civil/commercial law reforms and digital-government expansion. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'11.3 Wadis and flash-flood risk inventory','Wadis are not permanent rivers but episodic flood channels. The danger is that a dry bed can become a high-energy flow path after intense rainfall upstream. Wadi hydrology is especially important for road design, hiking safety, rural settlements, dams, tourism projects and utility corridors.

| Wadi / Area | Emirate / Region | Professional significance |
|---|---|---|
| Wadi Ham | Fujairah / east coast | Among the major drainage systems into Fujairah; relevant to road crossings, flood channels and rural development. |
| Wadi Wurayah | Fujairah | Protected mountain area with biodiversity value; development should consider conservation status and access controls. |
| Wadi Shawka | Ras Al Khaimah | Popular recreation area; flash flood and access-road design considerations. |
| Wadi Hatta | Dubai enclave | Hatta Dam, tourism, mountain recreation and hydrological sensitivity. |
| Wadi Al Helo | Sharjah / east-coast approach | Heritage and mountain-route context; road drainage and tourism safety relevant. |
| Wadi Bih / Jebel Jais drainage | RAK | Steep catchments, road gradients, tourism routes and rockfall/flood risk. |
| Al Ain / Jebel Hafeet wadis | Abu Dhabi | Mountain-foot alluvial fans, archaeology, heritage and road drainage. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'11.4 Islands and coastal development reference','| Island / Coastal Asset | Emirate | Main use / status | Professional notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Saadiyat Island | Abu Dhabi | Cultural district, museums, residential, hospitality, NYUAD | Sensitive coastal and cultural district; major master-planned development. |
| Yas Island | Abu Dhabi | Tourism, F1 circuit, theme parks, hospitality, residential | High event-footfall and traffic planning requirements. |
| Al Maryah Island | Abu Dhabi | ADGM financial district, healthcare, commercial | International financial free-zone overlay. |
| Sir Bani Yas | Abu Dhabi | Wildlife reserve and tourism | Conservation and heritage sensitivity. |
| Dalma | Abu Dhabi | Historic island community and pearl heritage | Transport, heritage and island utility issues. |
| Das / Zirku / Arzanah | Abu Dhabi offshore | Hydrocarbon logistics and processing | Industrial safety, marine environmental controls, offshore emergency planning. |
| Palm Jumeirah | Dubai | Artificial island, residential, hotel, tourism | Reclaimed-land settlement, coastal hydraulics, traffic and utilities. |
| Palm Jebel Ali | Dubai | Large artificial island redevelopment | Long-horizon master planning and infrastructure staging. |
| The World Islands | Dubai | Artificial island archipelago | Marine logistics, dredging, breakwater and private-island utilities. |
| Bluewaters | Dubai | Mixed-use island and tourism | Bridge/transport interface and marine edge design. |
| Abu Musa | Claimed by Sharjah / occupied by Iran | Disputed island | Sovereignty dispute; do not treat as operationally available UAE jurisdiction. |
| Greater Tunb / Lesser Tunb | Claimed by RAK / occupied by Iran | Disputed islands | Sovereignty dispute; official UAE position should be checked on MOFA sources. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'12.1 Archaeological and pre-Islamic periods','| Period | Approximate dates | Evidence / sites | Why it matters |
|---|---:|---|---|
| Neolithic coastal settlements | c. 6000–3500 BCE | Coastal shell middens, early fishing settlements | Establishes long-term coastal adaptation before modern ports and cities. |
| Hafit period | c. 3200–2600 BCE | Jebel Hafeet beehive tombs, Al Ain region | Important for archaeology, heritage protection and tourism planning. |
| Umm Al Nar culture | c. 2600–2000 BCE | Circular tombs, copper trade, Umm Al Nar island, Hili | Shows early long-distance trade with Mesopotamia, Oman and Indus Valley. |
| Wadi Suq period | c. 2000–1300 BCE | Weapons, ceramics, burials | Transitional period in settlement and trade patterns. |
| Iron Age | c. 1300–300 BCE | Falaj irrigation, fortified sites, Al Ain oasis systems | Irrigation heritage influences UNESCO and oasis conservation. |
| Mleiha / Hellenistic-Parthian period | c. 300 BCE–300 CE | Mleiha, coinage, caravan trade | Important for Sharjah archaeology and cultural-tourism projects. |
| Late antique / early Christian period | c. 5th–7th centuries CE | Sir Bani Yas monastery | Evidence of religious and trade diversity before Islam. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'12.2 Islamic, Portuguese and Qawasim maritime periods','The region entered Islamic history during the early Islamic expansion. Dibba is traditionally associated with one of the major Ridda Wars battles after the death of Prophet Muhammad. In later centuries, the ports of the southeastern Arabian coast connected the Gulf, East Africa, Persia and the Indian Ocean. Portuguese involvement from the 16th century created fortified coastal nodes and conflict around Hormuz and the approaches to the Gulf. By the 18th century, the Qawasim of Ras Al Khaimah and Sharjah became a major maritime power; British records described this as piracy, while regional narratives frame it as commercial and naval rivalry in an Indian Ocean context.','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'12.3 Trucial States treaty chronology','| Year | Event | Professional / institutional relevance |
|---:|---|---|
| 1820 | General Maritime Treaty between Britain and Gulf rulers | Start of the treaty framework that later produced the term Trucial States. |
| 1835 | Maritime truce arrangements | Seasonal truce developed into longer peace arrangements. |
| 1853 | Perpetual Maritime Truce | Formalised British maritime security order along the coast. |
| 1892 | Exclusive Agreements | Britain controlled external relations; rulers retained internal authority. |
| 1930s | Oil concessions begin | Petroleum concession law and state finance began to shape modern institutions. |
| 1952 | Trucial States Council established | Early inter-emirate administrative cooperation. |
| 1968 | Britain announces withdrawal east of Suez | Immediate trigger for federation negotiations. |
| 1971 | UAE founded on 2 December | Federal state established; RAK joined in February 1972. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'13.1 Federal structure','The UAE is a federation in which federal authority and emirate authority coexist. Federal competences cover foreign affairs, defence, security, nationality, immigration, currency, federal taxation, communications, air traffic control, labour and other matters listed in the Constitution. Residual powers remain with the emirates. In practice, Abu Dhabi and Dubai have especially extensive local institutional systems, while the federal government provides common rules for labour, immigration, civil transactions, corporate tax, VAT, criminal law, data protection and many strategic policies.','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'13.2 Supreme Council, President and Cabinet','| Institution | Composition | Main functions | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Federal Supreme Council | Rulers of the seven emirates | Supreme constitutional authority; elects President and Vice President; approves major federal matters | Abu Dhabi and Dubai rulers are central to quorum and political practice. |
| President | Elected by Supreme Council from among rulers | Head of state, federal decrees, appointments, representation | Historically the Ruler of Abu Dhabi. |
| Vice Presidents | Elected / appointed according to federal arrangements | Support federal executive leadership | Dubai ruler serves as Vice President and Prime Minister in current practice. |
| Council of Ministers / Cabinet | Prime Minister, deputies, ministers and ministers of state | Federal executive authority; policies, strategies, federal services | Cabinet portfolios are frequently updated; live cabinet list should be verified before publication use. |
| Federal National Council | 40 members, half elected through electoral colleges and half appointed | Advisory parliamentary role; reviews laws and questions ministers | Not a party-based legislature; political parties are not part of UAE system. |
| Federal Supreme Court | Federal constitutional and high judicial authority | Constitutional disputes, federal matters, inter-emirate disputes, selected criminal matters | Dubai and Abu Dhabi maintain local judicial systems for many matters. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'13.3 Practical implications of federalism','A professional working in the UAE must separate four layers of authority: federal laws, emirate laws, free-zone regimes, and sector regulators. A business may be licensed in a free zone but still need federal tax registration, MOHRE or free-zone employment compliance, customs registration, banking KYC, VAT/corporate tax filings, and sector-specific permissions. A construction project may sit under Dubai Municipality, Trakhees, DMT, a free-zone authority, or a local municipality, while also needing Civil Defence, utility, transport, environmental and property approvals.','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'14.1 Macro-economic structure','The UAE economy combines a large hydrocarbon base, sovereign capital, free-zone trade, aviation, ports, tourism, real estate, financial services, renewable energy and advanced-technology policy. Abu Dhabi remains the hydrocarbons and sovereign-investment centre; Dubai is the trade, tourism, aviation, logistics, finance and real-estate hub; Sharjah, RAK, Fujairah and the smaller emirates provide manufacturing, culture, ports, quarrying, affordable housing, free-zone and SME ecosystems.

| Sector | Main emirates / institutions | Reference issues |
|---|---|---|
| Oil and gas | Abu Dhabi, ADNOC, offshore islands, Ruwais | Concession terms, environmental permits, sour gas, LNG, downstream petrochemicals. |
| Trade and logistics | Dubai, JAFZA, DP World, Jebel Ali, DWC, KIZAD, Fujairah | Customs, free-zone licensing, port health, import permits, bonded warehouses. |
| Aviation | Dubai Airports, Emirates, flydubai, Etihad, Air Arabia, GCAA | Airline regulation, airport expansion, air cargo, slot coordination, drone rules. |
| Tourism and hospitality | Dubai, Abu Dhabi, RAK, Sharjah, Fujairah | Hotel classification, liquor permits, event permits, tourism fees, brand standards. |
| Real estate | Dubai DLD/RERA, ADREC, freehold zones | Escrow, Oqood, Ejari/Tawtheeq, rental disputes, service charges. |
| Finance | DIFC, ADGM, CBUAE, DFSA, FSRA, CMA, VARA | Banking licences, fund management, capital markets, virtual assets, AML/CFT. |
| Manufacturing | JAFZA, KIZAD, RAKEZ, Hamriyah, Sharjah industrial zones | Industrial licences, EHS, customs, standards, ICV and local-content programmes. |
| Renewable energy | Masdar, DEWA, EWEC, solar parks | PPAs, grid connection, land, utility procurement, carbon reporting. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'14.2 Sovereign wealth and state-owned enterprise ecosystem','| Institution | Role | Typical sectors |
|---|---|---|
| ADIA | Long-term global investment authority for Abu Dhabi | Public equities, fixed income, real estate, private equity, infrastructure. |
| Mubadala | Strategic investment company | Technology, aerospace, healthcare, energy, semiconductors, renewables. |
| ADQ | Abu Dhabi holding company for domestic strategic assets | Ports, food, healthcare, transport, utilities, industrial assets. |
| ICD | Dubai investment holding company | Emirates Group, banking, real estate, transport and Dubai strategic assets. |
