| AD Ports Group | Abu Dhabi ports and logistics | Ports, maritime, industrial zones, logistics, economic zones. |
| DP World | Dubai-origin global ports and logistics operator | Ports, terminals, logistics parks, trade corridors. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('company','Identity',null,'What is QuotesDone?','QuotesDone is a UAE B2B anonymous quotation marketplace. A buyer posts a request for quote (RFQ) describing a job or product, verified UAE suppliers submit quotes, and those quotes reach the buyer anonymised so the supplier''s identity is hidden. The buyer then pays a small reveal fee to unlock and contact a chosen supplier.','company canon Identity','COMPANY_CANON'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'14.3 Trade, customs and CEPA orientation','The UAE''s trade model relies on import, storage, re-export, value-added logistics, free-zone warehousing, gold and precious-stones trading, electronics, automotive re-export, food logistics and regional distribution. Dubai''s Jebel Ali and JAFZA are central to this model, but Abu Dhabi''s Khalifa Port/KIZAD and Fujairah''s bunkering and east-coast port system are also strategic. Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreements (CEPAs) with India and other states are designed to reduce tariffs, improve investment access and make the UAE a hub for regional trade.','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'15.1 Population pattern','The UAE has one of the world''s highest expatriate shares. This creates a labour market with a small national population, a large private-sector expatriate workforce, and significant household, construction, hospitality, logistics, retail and professional-service labour segments. Demographic statistics vary by source and by whether they count residents, nationals, migrant workers, dependants or census-enumerated population. For official work, use FCSC, emirate statistics centres or the relevant regulator, and mark estimates clearly.

| Population feature | Practical meaning |
|---|---|
| High expatriate share | Labour, housing, schools, healthcare and immigration rules are central to business planning. |
| Male-skewed workforce | Construction, logistics, transport and industrial labour drive demographic gender imbalance. |
| Emiratisation policy | Private-sector firms above thresholds face quota and penalty systems; NAFIS provides incentives. |
| Multi-curriculum education | British, Indian, American, IB, French, Pakistani, Filipino and MoE curricula create a complex school market. |
| Transient residence | Visa status, employment status and sponsor arrangements affect tenancy, banking, healthcare and schooling. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'15.2 Labour force sector map','| Sector | Common workforce characteristics | Regulatory interfaces |
|---|---|---|
| Construction | Large expatriate labour force, subcontractors, site accommodation | MOHRE, WPS, municipality, Civil Defence, OHS, accommodation standards. |
| Hospitality and F&B | Shift work, service staff, alcohol/hotel rules, food safety | DET/DTCM, municipality food safety, MOHRE, immigration, Civil Defence. |
| Domestic work | Separate domestic worker law and recruitment agencies | MOHRE/domestic worker framework, ICP, recruitment agency rules. |
| Finance and professional services | High-skilled expatriates, free-zone employment contracts | DIFC/ADGM employment law, CBUAE/DFSA/FSRA, MOHRE depending on jurisdiction. |
| Logistics and transport | Drivers, port workers, warehouse staff | RTA/MOEI/GCAA/ports/customs, MOHRE, OHS. |
| Retail | Large mall workforce, variable hours, part-time work | MOHRE, mall operator rules, consumer protection, VAT. |','legal UAE General Information','UAE_General_Information.md'),
('legal','UAE_General_Information',null,'16.1 Roads and tolling','| Road / System | Description | Professional use |
